"Natural indigo dye specimen no.7 | Science Museum Group Collection (https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co9382056/natural-indigo-dye-specimen-no-7)\nciteturn2151view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co9382056/natural-indigo-dye-specimen-no-7\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 195\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Image: Science Museum Group L3: \nL4: [Button: Collection]\nL5:   * cite2†Collection L6:   * cite3†Learning†learning.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk L7:   * cite4†The group†www.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk L8:   * cite5†Visit us Expand child menu\nL9:     * cite6†Locomotion†www.locomotion.org.uk L10:     * cite7†National Railway Museum†www.railwaymuseum.org.uk L11:     * cite8†National Science and Media Museum†www.scienceandmediamuseum.org.uk L12:     * cite9†Science and Industry Museum†www.scienceandindustrymuseum.org.uk L13:     * cite10†Science and Innovation Park†www.scienceinnovationpark.org.uk L14:     * cite11†Science Museum†www.sciencemuseum.org.uk L15: # cite2†Collection L16: \nL17:   * cite12†Search L18:   * cite13†About L19: \nL20:   * cite12†Search L21:   * cite13†About L22:   * cite2†Collection L23:   * cite3†Learning†learning.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk L24:   * cite4†The group†www.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk L25:   * cite5†Visit us Expand child menu\nL26:     * cite6†Locomotion†www.locomotion.org.uk L27:     * cite7†National Railway Museum†www.railwaymuseum.org.uk L28:     * cite8†National Science and Media Museum†www.scienceandmediamuseum.org.uk L29:     * cite9†Science and Industry Museum†www.scienceandindustrymuseum.org.uk L30:     * cite10†Science and Innovation Park†www.scienceinnovationpark.org.uk L31:     * cite11†Science Museum†www.sciencemuseum.org.uk L32: [Input: Search objects, people, categories, object numbers etc.]\nL33: # Natural indigo dye specimen no.7\nL34: \nL35: Made:\nL36:     unknown date in unknown\nL37: \nL38: maker:\nL39:     Unknown\nL40: \nL41: Specimen of natural indigo with inscriptions: \"E.M. / DABEEPOR / No. 7\", produced from the leaves of indigofera tinctoria.\nL42: Indigo refers to the blue substance, used as a dye or pigment, extracted from the leaves of various plants. There are hundreds of plants that can be used to produce indigo dye, including the Indigofera tinctoria plant which was used to produce this sample. Woad, Isatis tinctoria, also produces an indigo dye, but is often differentiated from dye produced by tropical and subtropical plants which produce much higher concentrations and are better suited to dying less absorbent fibers such as cotton.\nL43: Large-scale indigo plantations began to emerge in the 17th century and could eventually be found in locations including the Caribbean, South and Central America, South Asia and the American South. These plantations were key in the global trade of indigo and the supply of this dye to Europe. They were often owned and supervised by Europeans and relied on the labour of enslaved people or local indentured workers. Many Indian farmers were also coerced into growing indigo instead of food crops.\nL44: To produce a block of indigo, such as this, cuttings from the plant would be bundled together and put into vats, covered with water and beaten until they began to ferment. Once fermentation began, the liquid was moved into another vat where it was churned until the dying particles separated from the liquid and formed a mass. The liquid would then be drained away and the residue would be dried, placed into boxes, cut into squares and left to fully dry and harden.\nL45: This process converted the indigo into a usable pigment that could be exported and shipped.\nL46: By the start of the 19th century, the province of Bengal (now Bangladesh and the Indian state of West Bengal) had emerged as the main global supplier. However, due to developments in synthetic dyes, by around the end of WWI (1918), synthetic indigo became more popular than its plant-based counterpart, reducing investment in natural indigo production.\nL47: \nL48: This indigo sample was gifted to the Science Museum by indigo importers Parsons and Keith.\nL49: ## Details\nL50: \nL51: Category:\nL52:     cite14†Industrial Chemistry L53: \nL54: Object Number:\nL55:     1914-659/1\nL56: \nL57: Materials:\nL58:     cite15†pigment L59: \nL60: type:\nL61:     cite16†dye , cite17†natural dye and cite18†indigo L62: \nL63: credit:\nL64:     Parsons and Keith\nL65: \nL66: ## Cite this page\nL67: \nL68: Text to use for citation Science Museum Group. Natural indigo dye specimen no.7. 1914-659/1 Science Museum Group Collection Online. Accessed 14 October 2026. https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co9382056/natural-indigo-dye-specimen-no-7.\nL69: ### Rights\nL70: \nL71: We encourage the use and reuse of our collection data.\nL72: \nL73: Data in the title, made, maker and details fields are released under cite19†Creative Commons Zero†creativecommons.org . Descriptions and all other text content are licensed under a cite20†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence†creativecommons.org L74: \nL75: cite21†Using our data†www.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk L76: ## Download\nL77: \nL78: Download catalogue entry as cite22†json (Javascript Object Notation) L79: \nL80: Download manifest cite23†IIIF L81: \nL82: Our records are constantly being enhanced and improved, but please note that we cannot guarantee the accuracy of any information shown on this website.\nL83: \nL84: ## Parts\nL85: ### Fragment of natural indigo specimen no. 7\nL86: \nL87: Fragment of natural indigo specimen with inscriptions: \"E.M. / DABEEPOR / No. 7\", produced from the leaves of indigofera tinctoria.\nL88: \nL89: More\nL90: Indigo refers to the blue substance, used as a dye or pigment, extracted from the leaves of various plants. There are hundreds of plants that can be used to produce indigo dye, including the Indigofera tinctoria plant which was used to produce this sample. Woad, Isatis tinctoria, also produces an indigo dye, but is often differentiated from dye produced by tropical and subtropical plants which produce much higher concentrations and are better suited to dying less absorbent fibers such as cotton.\nL91: Large-scale indigo plantations began to emerge in the 17th century and could eventually be found in locations including the Caribbean, South and Central America, South Asia and the American South. These plantations were key in the global trade of indigo and the supply of this dye to Europe. They were often owned and supervised by Europeans and relied on the labour of enslaved people or local indentured workers. Many Indian farmers were also coerced into growing indigo instead of food crops.\nL92: To produce a block of indigo, such as this, cuttings from the plant would be bundled together and put into vats, covered with water and beaten until they began to ferment. Once fermentation began, the liquid was moved into another vat where it was churned until the dying particles separated from the liquid and formed a mass. The liquid would then be drained away and the residue would be dried, placed into boxes, cut into squares and left to fully dry and harden.\nL93: This process converted the indigo into a usable pigment that could be exported and shipped.\nL94: By the start of the 19th century, the province of Bengal (now Bangladesh and the Indian state of West Bengal) had emerged as the main global supplier. However, due to developments in synthetic dyes, by around the end of WWI (1918), synthetic indigo became more popular than its plant-based counterpart, reducing investment in natural indigo production.\nL95: \nL96: This indigo sample was gifted to the Science Museum by indigo importers Parsons and Keith.\nL97: \nL98: Measurements:\nL99:     overall: 25 x 80 x 50 mm\nL100: Materials:\nL101:     cite15†pigment L102: \nL103: Object Number:\nL104:     1914-659/1/1\nL105: \nL106: type:\nL107:     cite16†dye , cite17†natural dye and cite18†indigo L108: \nL109: Image ©\nL110:     The Board of Trustees of the Science Museum\nL111: \nL112: Fragment of natural indigo specimen with inscriptions: \"E.M. / DABEEPOR / No. 7\", produced from the leaves of indigofera tinctoria.\nL113: ### Fragment of natural indigo specimen no. 7\nL114: \nL115: Fragment of natural indigo specimen with inscriptions: \"E.M. / DABEEPOR / No. 7\", produced from the leaves of indigofera tinctoria.\nL116: \nL117: More\nL118: Indigo refers to the blue substance, used as a dye or pigment, extracted from the leaves of various plants. There are hundreds of plants that can be used to produce indigo dye, including the Indigofera tinctoria plant which was used to produce this sample. Woad, Isatis tinctoria, also produces an indigo dye, but is often differentiated from dye produced by tropical and subtropical plants which produce much higher concentrations and are better suited to dying less absorbent fibers such as cotton.\nL119: Large-scale indigo plantations began to emerge in the 17th century and could eventually be found in locations including the Caribbean, South and Central America, South Asia and the American South. These plantations were key in the global trade of indigo and the supply of this dye to Europe. They were often owned and supervised by Europeans and relied on the labour of enslaved people or local indentured workers. Many Indian farmers were also coerced into growing indigo instead of food crops.\nL120: To produce a block of indigo, such as this, cuttings from the plant would be bundled together and put into vats, covered with water and beaten until they began to ferment. Once fermentation began, the liquid was moved into another vat where it was churned until the dying particles separated from the liquid and formed a mass. The liqu