Asia and the American South. These plantations were key in the global trade of indigo and the supply of this dye to Europe. They were often owned and supervised by Europeans and relied on the labour of enslaved people or local indentured workers. Many Indian farmers were also coerced into growing indigo instead of food crops.\nL145: To produce a block of indigo, such as this, cuttings from the plant would be bundled together and put into vats, covered with water and beaten until they began to ferment. Once fermentation began, the liquid was moved into another vat where it was churned until the dying particles separated from the liquid and formed a mass. The liquid would then be drained away and the residue would be dried, placed into boxes, cut into squares and left to fully dry and harden.\nL146: This process converted the indigo into a usable pigment that could be exported and shipped.\nL147: By the start of the 19th century, the province of Bengal (now Bangladesh and the Indian state of West Bengal) had emerged as the main global supplier. However, due to developments in synthetic dyes, by around the end of WWI (1918), synthetic indigo became more popular than its plant-based counterpart, reducing investment in natural indigo production.\nL148: \nL149: This indigo sample was gifted to the Science Museum by indigo importers Parsons and Keith.\nL150: Measurements:\nL151:     left and right sections: 75 mm x 75 mm x 75 mm,\nL152: left section: 75 mm x 60 mm x 75 mm,\nL153: \nL154: Materials:\nL155:     cite15†pigment L156: \nL157: Object Number:\nL158:     1914-659/1/3\nL159: \nL160: type:\nL161:     cite16†dye and cite17†natural dye L162: \nL163: Right section of natural indigo specimen with inscriptions: \"E.M. / DABEEPOR / No. 7\", produced from the leaves of indigofera tinctoria.\nL164: ### Right section of natural indigo dye specimen no.7\nL165: \nL166: Right section of natural indigo specimen with inscriptions: \"E.M. / DABEEPOR / No. 7\", produced from the leaves of indigofera tinctoria.\nL167: \nL168: More\nL169: Indigo refers to the blue substance, used as a dye or pigment, extracted from the leaves of various plants. There are hundreds of plants that can be used to produce indigo dye, including the Indigofera tinctoria plant which was used to produce this sample. Woad, Isatis tinctoria, also produces an indigo dye, but is often differentiated from dye produced by tropical and subtropical plants which produce much higher concentrations and are better suited to dying less absorbent fibers such as cotton.\nL170: Large-scale indigo plantations began to emerge in the 17th century and could eventually be found in locations including the Caribbean, South and Central America, South Asia and the American South. These plantations were key in the global trade of indigo and the supply of this dye to Europe. They were often owned and supervised by Europeans and relied on the labour of enslaved people or local indentured workers. Many Indian farmers were also coerced into growing indigo instead of food crops.\nL171: To produce a block of indigo, such as this, cuttings from the plant would be bundled together and put into vats, covered with water and beaten until they began to ferment. Once fermentation began, the liquid was moved into another vat where it was churned until the dying particles separated from the liquid and formed a mass. The liquid would then be drained away and the residue would be dried, placed into boxes, cut into squares and left to fully dry and harden.\nL172: This process converted the indigo into a usable pigment that could be exported and shipped.\nL173: By the start of the 19th century, the province of Bengal (now Bangladesh and the Indian state of West Bengal) had emerged as the main global supplier. However, due to developments in synthetic dyes, by around the end of WWI (1918), synthetic indigo became more popular than its plant-based counterpart, reducing investment in natural indigo production.\nL174: \nL175: This indigo sample was gifted to the Science Museum by indigo importers Parsons and Keith.\nL176: Measurements:\nL177:     left and right sections: 75 mm x 75 mm x 75 mm,\nL178: right section: 63 mm x 44 mm x 67 mm,\nL179: \nL180: Materials:\nL181:     cite15†pigment L182: \nL183: Object Number:\nL184:     1914-659/1/4\nL185: \nL186: type:\nL187:     cite16†dye and cite17†natural dye L188: ## The Science Museum Group\nL189: \nL190: cite6†Locomotion†www.locomotion.org.uk cite7†National Railway Museum†www.railwaymuseum.org.uk cite8†National Science and Media Museum†www.scienceandmediamuseum.org.uk cite9†Science and Industry Museum†www.scienceandindustrymuseum.org.uk cite10†Science and Innovation Park†www.scienceinnovationpark.org.uk cite11†Science Museum†www.sciencemuseum.org.uk L191:   * cite24†Privacy and cookies†group.sciencemuseum.org.uk L192:   * cite25†Terms and conditions†group.sciencemuseum.org.uk L193:   * cite26†Web accessibility†www.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk L194: cite27†Back to Top --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2151view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://faculty.ksu.edu.sa/sites/default/files/1_qualitative_tests_of_amino_acids__1.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://faculty.ksu.edu.sa/sites/default/files/1_qualitative_tests_of_amino_acids__1.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2151view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.rlbcau.ac.in/pdf/Forestry/FBS-143%20Plant%20Biochemistry.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://www.rlbcau.ac.in/pdf/Forestry/FBS-143%20Plant%20Biochemistry.pdf is not accessible via this tool.\n"
